The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003) and 1850 (2008),
Reaffirming its commitment to a just, lasting and comprehensive peace in the Middle East based on the vision of two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, and stressing that final status issues, including Jerusalem, must be resolved through negotiations between the parties,
Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter,
Deploring the recent loss of civilian life and injuries in Gaza and southern Israel, and expressing grave concern at the recent escalation of violence,
Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including the firing of rockets and mortars from Gaza into Israel by Hamas and other militant groups, and all acts of terror, and stressing the need for all parties to respect international humanitarian law and to protect civilians, medical personnel and humanitarian workers,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Gaza and stressing the need for urgent and sustained humanitarian assistance, including medical evacuations and safe and unhindered access for humanitarian personnel,
Recognizing the efforts of the United Nations, Egypt and other regional and international partners to de-escalate the situation and restore calm,
Stressing the importance of preserving the status quo at the holy sites in Jerusalem and respecting the special role of the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan,
Stressing the need for calm and de-escalation and for all parties to refrain from incitement and from actions that undermine confidence and the viability of the two-State solution,
1. Condemns all acts of violence against civilians, including the firing of rockets and mortars from Gaza into Israel by Hamas and other militant groups, and calls for the immediate cessation of all such acts and for calm and de-escalation;
2. Calls upon all parties to exercise maximum restraint, to avoid civilian casualties, to respect international humanitarian law and to ensure the protection of civilians, including women and children, medical personnel and humanitarian workers;
3. Calls upon the Palestinian Authority and all Palestinian factions to prevent attacks, incitement and the launching of incendiary devices, and to cooperate in restoring calm;
4. Calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that could escalate tensions and to take steps to rebuild confidence and restore calm, including through cooperation with the United Nations and Egypt;
5. Expresses grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Gaza and calls for immediate and sustained humanitarian access, including through crossings opened for humanitarian and medical purposes, medical evacuations and the work of United Nations agencies and humanitarian partners;
6. Welcomes the efforts of Egypt and the United Nations Special Coordinator for the Middle East Peace Process to consolidate a ceasefire and restore calm, and encourages further diplomatic efforts by the Secretary-General and regional partners;
7. Reaffirms its support for a negotiated two-State solution and calls upon the parties to resume credible negotiations on all final status issues, including Jerusalem, borders, security, refugees and water, and to refrain from actions that undermine confidence and the viability of the two-State solution;
8. Calls for transparent and credible investigations by the relevant authorities into incidents involving civilian casualties and for accountability, in accordance with international standards;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including the humanitarian situation in Gaza and efforts to restore calm;
10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions, including resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008) and 2334 (2016),
Reaffirming its commitment to a just, lasting and comprehensive peace in the Middle East based on the vision of two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, living side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, and stressing that final status issues, including Jerusalem, must be resolved through negotiations between the parties, and that no unilateral actions can prejudge the outcome of final status negotiations,
Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, and stressing that this right does not justify disproportionate or indiscriminate use of force against civilians,
Reaffirming the inadmissibility of the acquisition of territory by force, recalling resolution 2334 (2016), including that Israeli settlements in the Palestinian territory occupied since 1967, including East Jerusalem, have no legal validity and constitute a major obstacle to the two-State solution, and reiterating the obligation under the Fourth Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, of 12 August 1949,
Deploring the recent loss of civilian life and injuries in Gaza and southern Israel, and expressing grave concern at the recent escalation of violence,
Condemning all acts of violence against civilians, including the firing of rockets and mortars from Gaza into Israel by Hamas and other militant groups, and all acts of terror, and stressing the need for all parties to respect international humanitarian law and to protect civilians, medical personnel and humanitarian workers,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Gaza and stressing the need for urgent and sustained humanitarian assistance, including medical evacuations and safe and unhindered access for humanitarian personnel,
Recognizing the efforts of the United Nations, Egypt and other regional and international partners to de-escalate the situation and restore calm,
Stressing the importance of preserving the historical status quo of Jerusalem and at the holy sites, and respecting the special role of the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan,
Stressing the need for calm and de-escalation and for all parties to refrain from incitement and from actions that undermine confidence and the viability of the two-State solution,
1. Condemns all acts of violence against civilians, including the firing of rockets and mortars from Gaza into Israel by Hamas and other militant groups, and the excessive, disproportionate or indiscriminate use of force by Israeli security forces against Palestinian civilians, including in the context of the Gaza border protests, and calls for the immediate cessation of all such acts and for calm and de-escalation; reaffirms Israel's right to self-defence in accordance with international law;
2. Calls upon all parties, including Israel as the occupying Power, to exercise maximum restraint, to avoid civilian casualties, to respect international humanitarian law and to ensure the protection of civilians, including women and children, medical personnel and journalists, and to refrain from excessive and lethal force against civilians, including peaceful protesters, and to ensure that any use of force is proportionate and in full compliance with international humanitarian law;
3. Calls upon the Palestinian Authority and all Palestinian factions to prevent attacks, incitement and the launching of incendiary devices, and to cooperate in restoring calm; and calls upon all parties, including Israel, to cease settlement activities and other unilateral actions that undermine calm and the two-State solution;
4. Calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that could escalate tensions and to take steps to rebuild confidence and restore calm, including through cooperation with the United Nations and Egypt;
5. Expresses grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Gaza and calls for immediate and sustained humanitarian access, including through the sustained opening of all crossings, including Kerem Shalom and Rafah, for humanitarian, medical and commercial purposes, medical evacuations and the work of United Nations agencies, including UNRWA, and humanitarian partners, and calls for the easing of restrictions on the movement of people and goods, consistent with security needs;
6. Reaffirms resolution 2334 (2016) and calls for the full implementation of its provisions, including the immediate cessation of all settlement activities in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, and full respect for legal obligations in this regard;
7. Welcomes the efforts of Egypt and the United Nations Special Coordinator for the Middle East Peace Process to consolidate a ceasefire and restore calm, and encourages further diplomatic efforts by the Secretary-General and regional partners;
8. Reaffirms its support for a negotiated two-State solution based on the 4 June 1967 lines, with agreed swaps, and in accordance with relevant United Nations resolutions, and calls upon the parties to resume credible negotiations on all final status issues, including Jerusalem, borders, security, refugees and water, and to refrain from actions that undermine confidence and the viability of the two-State solution, including settlement activities, incitement and unilateral actions that prejudge final status issues;
9. Calls upon Israel to respect the right to peaceful protest and to refrain from the use of live ammunition except as a last resort; calls for independent, transparent and credible investigations into all incidents involving civilian casualties, including the use of live fire by Israeli forces during the Gaza border protests and the firing of rockets and mortars from Gaza, and for accountability in accordance with international standards;
10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including the humanitarian situation in Gaza, efforts to restore calm and the implementation of resolution 2334 (2016);
11. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

